/**
\page pointer_patching Pointer Patching

`EEObjectPtr<T>` is a lazy handle to an `EEObject`. It stores a GUID at all
times and, once resolved, also stores a `std::shared_ptr<T>`. The GUID is the
serialized identity; the shared pointer is a cache that can be rebuilt by the
asset registry.

`IEEObjectPtr` is the non-typed interface used by the reflection layer. It
exposes `GetGuid()` and `SetGuid()` so the `EngineDataType::ObjectPtr` codec can
capture a GUID without knowing `T`. There is currently no virtual `Deserialize()`
on the interface.

\section pointer_patching_states Handle states

- **Resolved:** `m_ptr` is non-null. `m_guid` mirrors `m_ptr->GetGuid()`.
- **Deferred:** `m_ptr` is null and `m_guid` is valid. The object is looked up on
  the first dereference.
- **Empty:** `m_ptr` is null and `m_guid` is invalid. Dereferencing returns
  `nullptr`.

Assigning a `shared_ptr`, calling `reset()`, or constructing from a
`shared_ptr` enters the resolved state. Constructing from a GUID, or calling
`SetGuid()` with a different GUID, clears `m_ptr` and enters the deferred state.

\section pointer_patching_flow Flow

\dot
digraph EEObjectPtrResolution {
  rankdir=LR;
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor="#f7f7f7", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10];
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#555555"];

  subgraph cluster_states {
    label="EEObjectPtr<T> states";
    color="#aaaaaa";

    resolved [label="Resolved\nm_ptr != null\nm_guid mirrors object"];
    deferred [label="Deferred\nm_ptr == null\nvalid m_guid"];
    empty [label="Empty / failed\ninvalid m_guid"];
  }

  subgraph cluster_capture {
    label="Serialization";
    color="#aaaaaa";

    capture [label="Serialize()\nor TypeLayout::CaptureState()"];
    guidvalue [label="GUID bytes\nor JSON string"];
    apply [label="TypeLayout::ApplyState()\nObjectPtr codec\nApply: TODO, logs error", fillcolor="#ffe4e4"];
  }

  subgraph cluster_resolve {
    label="Lazy resolution";
    color="#aaaaaa";

    deref [label="get() / -> / *\nbool / ToShared()"];
    hasptr [shape=diamond, label="m_ptr?"];
    validguid [shape=diamond, label="m_guid valid?"];
    helper [label="Detail::ResolveAssetHelper()"];
    reference [label="Detail::ResolveAssetReference()"];
    castback [label="static_pointer_cast<T>()\nthen UpdateGuid()"];
  }

  subgraph cluster_registry {
    label="AssetRegistry";
    color="#aaaaaa";

    index [label="s_indexedAssets\nRegisterAsset() / RegisterAssetOnDisk()\nRefreshFromDisk() reads m_guid"];
    indexed [shape=diamond, label="indexed?"];
    cached [label="cached instance\nreturn as Asset"];
    load [label="isOnDisk\nLoadAssetFromDisk()\nfactory + JSON + SetGuid + OnLoad"];
  }

  assign [label="shared_ptr / reset()"];
  guidset [label="Guid ctor / SetGuid()"];
  assign -> resolved;
  guidset -> deferred;

  resolved -> capture;
  capture -> guidvalue;
  guidvalue -> apply [label="JSON only", color="#cc3333", fontcolor="#cc3333"];

  resolved -> deref;
  deferred -> deref;
  empty -> deref;

  deref -> hasptr;
  hasptr -> resolved [label="yes: return"];
  hasptr -> validguid [label="no"];
  validguid -> empty [label="no"];
  validguid -> helper [label="yes"];

  helper -> reference;
  reference -> indexed;
  index -> indexed;
  indexed -> cached [label="yes + loaded"];
  indexed -> load [label="yes / not loaded"];
  indexed -> empty [label="no"];

  cached -> castback;
  load -> castback;
  castback -> resolved [label="found"];
  castback -> empty [label="missing / wrong type"];
}
\enddot

\section pointer_patching_steps Steps

1. On capture, `EEObjectPtr<T>::Serialize()` writes the GUID of the resolved
   object, or the stored GUID when deferred. The reflection codec writes the
   same GUID as a string in `PropertyField::Value`.
2. On first dereference of a deferred handle, `get()` calls
   `Detail::ResolveAssetHelper(guid)`.
3. `ResolveAssetHelper()` delegates to `ResolveAssetReference()`, which calls
   `AssetRegistry::GetAsset<Asset>(guid)` and casts the result to `EEObject`.
4. `AssetRegistry` returns a cached instance when one exists. Otherwise, for an
   on-disk entry, it creates the registered type through `TypeRegistry`,
   deserializes the JSON file, restores the GUID from the indexed `m_guid`, and
   calls `OnLoad()`.
5. `EEObjectPtr` statically casts the `EEObject` back to `T` and calls
   `UpdateGuid()`. A successful lookup caches the result in `m_ptr`; a failed
   lookup leaves `m_ptr` null and clears `m_guid`.

\warning Only `Asset`-derived objects can be resolved this way. `EEObject`
constructors generate a GUID but do not register the object globally; the old
`ObjectRegistry` design no longer exists. An `EEObjectPtr<GameObject>` that is
only assigned a GUID cannot be rebuilt unless the target is indexed as an asset.

\warning Restoring `EngineDataType::ObjectPtr` from JSON is not implemented.
`ValueCodec::Apply` logs an error and returns `false`, so a deferred pointer
must receive its GUID through code (`Guid` constructor or `SetGuid()`) before
lazy resolution can run.

\note A failed lookup is terminal for that handle: `UpdateGuid()` resets the
GUID when `m_ptr` is null, so subsequent dereferences do not retry the registry.
*/
